# Designing for 2043: What Terminal F Should Absorb Before the Concrete Cures

**Draft:** Final (remediated), 2026-08-19  
**Audience:** DFW Airport Board, CEO's office, capital planners; peer executives and policy readers

---

## Opening

August 2026, overnight, at the north end of the DFW terminal area: a steel-and-glass box 278 feet long, 136 feet across, and weighing 3,320 tons creeps down a closed taxiway on self-propelled modular transporters.[^1] Six mega-modules make the trip inside a two-week overnight window; the contractor calls the largest the biggest ever moved for a terminal expansion.[^1]

By the time that module makes the trip, its column grid is fixed. Its floor-to-floor height is fixed. Its MEP risers, its baggage-belt penetrations, its structural allowance for whatever might ever sit above it — all fixed, months earlier, in a factory. The board has never seen those decisions as a decision list.

That is the first thing wrong with the phrase "before the concrete cures." The operative deadline is release-for-fabrication, and it runs module by module. For the six modules moved in August 2026, it has passed.[^2] For the FIS, the Skylink node, and the Phase 2 apron modules that would carry MARS provisions, it has not — but no one outside Innovation Next+ has the calendar on paper. Getting it is the register's first act of governance.

The thesis takes three sentences. American Airlines — Terminal F's sole tenant on a lease that runs to 2043[^3] — has already declared Terminal F is not its premium anchor: largest-ever Admirals Club to Terminal C, Flagship Check-In to Terminal D, a Provisions grab-and-go to Terminal F.[^4] The historical record on purpose-built single-carrier hub terminals says the buildings that survived a strategy shift did so by encoding generic hub *form*, not carrier-specific finishes.[^5] The correct response is neither to override the tenant with premium bones it did not ask for, nor to accept the announced program as the whole 2043 program: it is a board-adopted reversibility register, paired with a U&L side letter, that locks in generic hub geometry — MARS-capable stands, an FIS sized to a commissioned 2043 international peak, a Skylink node that survives a single-loop morning — and preserves a small, priced list of Flagship-convertible shell options American can trigger on its own balance sheet when it is ready.

