## The argument

### The tenant has already told you what Terminal F is

In July 2026 American rolled out its "next-generation premium experience at DFW": the 37,000-square-foot Admirals Club in Terminal C adjacent to Skylink, its largest club anywhere; Flagship Check-In in Terminal D near the D30 checkpoint; and for Terminal F, a Provisions by Admirals Club grab-and-go for short-layover transit passengers.[^4] Not a Flagship Lounge. Not a full Admirals Club.

This is the party with the most information about its own 2043 network telling DFW where the premium program lives, and it is not the terminal DFW is now building.

The run prompt names American's premium pivot as the reason to design Terminal F to absorb it. Right about the pivot, wrong about the terminal. The pivot is a structural yield play with physical implications — widebody gate mix, lounge seat pitch, premium check-in envelope — but American is executing it through Terminals C and D.[^4] [^6] Terminal F is being scoped as a connecting building with a grab-and-go.

The register has to survive both defensible readings of the Provisions signal. The *tactical* reading: Provisions is a live 2026 capital-allocation signal, not a settled 2043 program, and the airport should preserve the envelope that lets American change its mind in 2032 without a nine-figure mid-lease retrofit. The *strategic* reading: the premium capital is permanently in Terminals C and D, Terminal F is connecting-only for the life of the lease, and the shell allowance is dead weight for 17 years — foregone lease revenue at approximately $100–150/sf/yr for comparable non-signatory terminal space (industry estimate; no DFW-specific rate is in the public record).[^18] On the ~19,000 sf in Card 3, that is roughly $1.9–2.9M a year, $32–49M over 17 years at 4%.[^18] Real money — and still four to six times smaller than the low-case at-grade retrofit displacement of $204M. The strategic reading raises the option's cost. It does not invert the arithmetic.

### The bank is getting denser, not flatter

American is doing a second thing that cuts against a premium reading of the Terminal F floor plate. In December 2025 it announced a restructure from 9 to 13 daily connecting banks starting April 2026 — "doubling down on DFW... further strengthens its Flagship hub," in its own framing.[^19] The 9 a.m. peak drops from ~110 to ~52 departures; 40-plus flights move into hours that used to be trough; scheduled block times lengthen by an average of 6 minutes across 145 markets to buy connection reliability.[^20]

Bank contiguity replaces peak-hour surge. Gate concurrency at Terminal F is now set by how tightly the 13 banks butt up against each other. That is connecting complexity, not a shift toward originating premium.

Two caveats. One defensible reading of the announcement is a *flatter*, mid-day-loaded operation, which would soften the density case. And the airline's reported 50% cut in missed connections is based on early post-launch results.[^21] The operational data is more sober: DFW's Q2 2026 departure rate is up ~1% year over year, still second-worst among busy US airports, and the +6-minute block additions mechanically inflate scheduled on-time performance.[^22] Size Terminal F to the unproven-benefit case, not the press release.

**The connecting-share number is contested and the design has to commit to one.** Airport-level reporting places connecting share around 60%; the run prompt asserts a carrier-level two-thirds.[^23] Between 55% and 70% connecting, peak-day originating swings by ~50% — 30,000 versus 45,000 O&D passenger-trips at American's ~100,000 peak-day.[^24] "Sized to hold either" is not a design decision. **The register's design basis is 62.5% connecting / 37.5% originating (peak-day O&D ~37,500), with curbside, ticketing hall depth, TSA lane count, and BHS spine sized to that midpoint and structurally oversized to hold 70% originator at an incremental ~$18–25M[^59]** — Card 6, Category A. That is a decision.

### History is not neutral about the shape of this building

The industry has run this play — a purpose-built hub terminal on a long single-carrier commitment — several times since deregulation. Four cases went badly when the carrier repositioned. Three speak affirmatively about a *building* absorbing a *strategy shift*.

Pittsburgh's Midfield Terminal opened October 1, 1992 at roughly $1 billion, its X-shaped airside optimized for the US Airways connecting bank. US Airways dehubbed in 2004 after a fee dispute. Cost per enplaned passenger rose to nearly $15 by 2011, roughly twice the 2002 rate.[^25] Pittsburgh spent fifteen years running a hub airport after the hub had left; stranded midfield debt lingered until roughly 2019; a new $1.75B landside terminal opened in 2025, essentially undoing the 1992 geometry.[^26] Cincinnati went from 22.7 million passengers and 600-plus daily flights in 2005 to under 6 million and under 180 daily by 2013.[^27] St. Louis fell from more than 500 daily American/TWA flights in July 2001 to 207 by 2003.[^28] Cleveland's Concourse D went dark in May 2014 after United's dehub; destinations fell from 61 to 20, daily departures from about 200 to 72.[^29]

The affirmative cases, ranked by sourcing strength:

**Heathrow Terminal 5** (2008): delivered on time within £4.3B under the T5 Agreement relational contract; a generic multi-satellite hub form that absorbed British Airways' post-merger repositioning, lounge program evolution, and A380 introduction across two decades — then failed at opening with ~42,000 mishandled bags because operational readiness was undersized.[^30] The form absorbed the strategy. Readiness is a separate line item.

**LaGuardia Terminal C** (2022): the four-concourse ~$4B program delivered roughly two years early under single-airline governance, with the caveat that ~$3.5B was Delta's capital and ~$500M Port Authority.[^31] Governance delivers when the accountable party holds the capital. At Terminal F, the airport, not the airline, holds the pen.

**Detroit McNamara** (2002): SmithGroup-designed linear midfield with satellite concourses for Northwest; Northwest merged into Delta in 2008; Delta kept and still operates the terminal.[^7] The design record is primary; cost and retrofit history rest on trade press, so treat dollar figures as directional. The lesson: hub form with no carrier-specific structural moves absorbs a change of tenant intent — though as a survivable-merger case, not a dehub-survival case; the successor kept the hub.

Two cautions. The dehub cases are survivorship-biased toward drama: DFW, ATL, DEN, ORD, IAH, MSP, DTW, SLC, SEA, PHX, and CLT all held their hub carriers through the consolidation wave,[^61] and the base rate for a super-hub of DFW's scale being dehubbed inside 20 years is low.[^32] And Pittsburgh's 80% single-carrier concentration on a small regional O&D floor is no analogue for DFW's 82.6% American share on a two-city economy of roughly eight million people, with regional output up to $71.1B per regional press (magnitude only).[^32]

So the warning applies to the *shape of the building*, not the *survival of the hub*. Encode Terminal F's shape generically enough to survive a strategy shift that history says will arrive, whether or not the carrier does.

### The operative deadline is release-for-fabrication, per module

"Before the concrete cures" is technically wrong for a modular build. The real deadline is "before the module is skinned" — and for the first six Phase 1 modules it has passed.[^1]

Modular construction does not suspend the physics of retrofit. Tolerance stack-up between modules fabricated on separate shifts, transported by SPMT, and placed to three-quarter-inch precision fails quietly: leaking curtain wall at the seam, misaligned take-away belts, life-safety commissioning findings — the failure modes that put Berlin Brandenburg nine years and at roughly 2.3× its original budget over.[^33] The ~30% savings figure is credible for on-site duration and reduced airside impact windows.[^8] It is not a reduction in the design-lock horizon.

**Category A — structurally irreversible once modules are set and utilities tied in.** Column grid and floor-to-floor heights. Sterile-corridor geometry into the FIS. Primary baggage tunnel alignment and depth. The apron column-and-jetway grid that determines whether a stand is Code C, Code E, or MARS. Hydrant fuel loop capacity and pit locations. Primary electrical service and standby generator sizing. 400 Hz and PCA capacity at the stand. The Skylink node, including platform-edge count and vertical circulation redundancy.

**Category B — reversible only at high schedule and airline-consent cost.** Lounge shell subdivisions inside preserved floor plate. Flagship arrival vestibule and dedicated elevator core. CBP booth counts within a preserved hall. TSA lane count within preserved queue geometry. BHS in-line EDS matrix within a preserved conveyor spine.

**Category C — cheap to defer, cheap to change later.** Interior finishes and wayfinding. Retail and concession fit-out. Furniture and lounge zoning. Low-voltage overlays — biometrics, digital signage, sensor networks — provided conduit, riser space, IDF closets, and PoE++ budget were sized generously in Category A.

The strongest, cheapest engineering move is to buy Category C flexibility by paying Category A prices in a small, named set of places. Oversize the risers. Oversize the IDF closets. Oversize the BHS spine. Preserve MARS provisions at a defined minority of stands. That is what "flexible" means when it is priced honestly.

### The 6:15 morning

Walk the building on a specific morning — a composite of ledger facts, since the airport-side design of the Terminal F Skylink station is not public.[^12]

05:55. Skylink is still running its overnight single-loop pattern — one direction, roughly 15-minute end-to-end transit, as it does nightly from ~22:00 to ~06:00.[^12] The first of American's 13 daily banks is already inbound. The restructured schedule's ~52-departure peak bank means on the order of 5,500 passengers at ~107 per departure, before any stranded fraction from the previous bank.[^34] Now hold that geometry against the last week of January 2023: winter storm ground stop, four-hour post-stop average delays, more than 1,100 cancellations on the peak day, more than 600 the day after.[^35]

Four chokepoints do the work. The Skylink node: platform edges, vertical circulation redundancy, concourse-side queue depth when the second loop fails to return at 06:00.[^12] Gate concurrency: how many bank-contiguous gates can turn simultaneously without spilling into taxilane object-free areas under FAA AC 150/5300-13B.[^36] MARS stands: the pressure valve that lets one position work a widebody international arrival or two narrowbody bank turns.[^11] And the FIS: CBP's ~100 pax/hr per double booth and 50–75 ft queue depth, against a schedule that targets Q3 2026 DFW capacity up ~6% while cutting LHR ~13%, with six new summer 2026 routes — Budapest, Prague, Athens, Milan, Zurich, expanded Buenos Aires.[^10] [^37]

Every one is a Category A decision locked at release-for-fabrication. If the Skylink station opens without a reserved second platform edge, redundant vertical circulation, and queuing sized for an unscheduled single-loop morning, the failure mode is not delayed connections. It is the connecting bank collapsing — the metric the airport and the airline both need to defend. If the FIS is sized to a 2025 international peak rather than a commissioned 2043 forecast, the airport buys the geometry back with CBP overtime for thirty years.

Premium at Terminal F, on this morning, is whatever survives the morning: reliable connections, a working Skylink node, an FIS that does not queue back into the sterile corridor. The Flagship-convertible shell rides along as a priced option, not as the headline.

### The register as an executable instrument

The register's authority rests on reversibility economics and on the asymmetry between DTW, LGA, and T5 on one side and Pittsburgh, STL, CVG, and CLE on the other.

**The register is a DFW Board policy resolution paired with a U&L side letter.** The resolution is the internal-authority instrument: a standing monthly review of Category A decisions against the fabrication calendar, board-approved disposition of any option before its last-responsible-moment date, and a $10M threshold[^54] above which an option requires a signed American cost-recovery memorandum before scope release. The side letter is the counterparty-binding instrument: American's concurrence on the four register options as a package, priced and dated, with rate treatment specified. Neither alone is sufficient. The resolution without the side letter is airport-side ambition; the side letter without the resolution has no internal calendar to enforce it.

Adoption requires 6 of 11 voting members at a regularly noticed board meeting; no city-council approval.[^38] The Fort Worth minority — 4 of 11 seats — is a real politics risk on any mid-program instrument perceived as favoring Dallas.[^38] The register's protection is that it is a discipline on cost, not scope, and it applies the same test to every option.

Two options belong on the register but need a settled contractual answer first. CUPPS-capable gate hardware — an open-standard shell allowing shared gate positions with swappable airline software — is IROPS insurance the operator wants;[^39] whether it survives an MII objection under the exclusive-use envelope belongs at the U&L amendment table. A gate-camera, Assaia-ready mounting envelope with sized PoE++ budget hedges turn-analytics deployment without betting on vendor ROI figures.[^40] If the U&L conversation slips past the Phase 2 tranche, fund the conduit and mounting envelope in airport-side base building and defer the active hardware.

The most important open question is not a design number but a contract number: whether the 2025 U&L extension obligates American to any Flagship-tier lounge presence at Terminal F before 2043.[^41] If yes, the shell is a design decision on the fabrication calendar. If no, it is a real-options decision at the ratemaking table.

