## Implications for the operator

The board digests one to three decisions per quarter. Presenting six as first-move equivalents is how it approves none of them.

**Q1 (next board cycle):** Adopt the register as a Board policy resolution and obtain the RFF schedule (Card 1). Open the U&L side-letter conversation on the four Category A options as a package (Cards 2–5).

**Q2:** Commission the 2043 international peak-hour forecast (Card 4). Confirm Skylink node design intent (Card 5) and the mezzanine-vs-at-grade siting assumption for the Flagship shell (Card 3).

**Q3:** Fund the operational-readiness discipline named since ACRP Synthesis 20 — operations seated at module-level factory design reviews with veto authority, witness testing at the factory, each module seam contracted as a mapped "operator interface plane" so MEP, low-voltage, and BHS branches can be re-terminated in 2038 without breaching structure.[^50] Deliverable: a written specification before the next module design lock, deposited in DFW's CMMS at commissioning. Adopt the O&M reserve policy.

**Terminal F O&M reserve policy.** A reserve sized to 1.5% of depreciated asset value annually,[^60] adopted concurrent with facility acceptance, treated as an above-the-line rate-base charge under the U&L. Baseline: BHS on a 15-year cycle at ~10% of purchase cost per year in EDS maintenance; HVAC and BMS at 15–20 years; first apron rehab in the 10–15 year window under AC 150/5380-7B; PBBs at 20–25 years with mid-life overhauls.[^51] Underfunding by 15% generates a nine-figure deferred-maintenance liability inside 20 years.[^60]

**Publishing the register externally is cut.** It would tip DFW's hand in every future rate conversation and create a downgrade trigger, against a rating benefit the evidence cannot measure. The register is internal-audit and board-materials only; selective disclosure in a rating cycle is a separate future decision.

